when it came to the duke even a brief  meeting could provide  a lifelong anecdote we asked people to  send us their memories  our inbox was inundated with stories  like  sonal dharmabandu's memories of salon  now sri lanka  in 1954 when the queen and prince philip  visited  the reception for the queen and prince  philip  was well underway i was eight  i managed to lose my way and lost my  parents  then came a policeman and took me by my  hand  and took me to the tent where the public  had the system was installed  so everything was stopped to make an  announcement  uh asking my parents to come and pick me  up so when my father came up  very close to where the queen and prince  william proceeded to take me  back to where they were i was not  interested in seeing the queen i was so  pleased to have been able to  see my parents back after a year where  there's been so much loss  and so much grief it might seem  startling  that the death of a 99 year old man  could cause so many people to stop  pause and reflect but perhaps it speaks  to the duke's longevity  to the breadth of his interests and his  character  that an encounter with him seems  difficult to forget  imam karyasi met him at buckingham  palace in 2005 with a group of other  faith leaders  it was an extraordinary meeting in that  that he used his  written wisdom to communicate he was  very considerate  but also very knowledgeable about other  religions  some didn't even have to meet the duke  to be struck by him  harry emmett from ackerington is a hobby  photographer  in 1977 harry and friends wrote to  buckingham palace  they asked if they could use some  wildlife photographs taken by the duke  in their camera club's annual exhibition  at this local art gallery  i received a phone call from norman  potter who was at the time curator  at the highway gallery who said  i've just had a phone call about you  from buckingham palace  you know the duke london buckingham  palace  i crington the fact that  we had been recognized and been trusted  to take these photographs  um it was quite a surprise yeah my  father was working out in the um in the  middle east of the iraq trailing company  around the early  1960s and him and his best friend were  doing up a boat  a flying 15 boat which required a total  overhaul so they needed some sales so  they wrote the  uk flying 15 association seeing if  anyone had any sales they could send out  uh and then about three or four weeks  later they got this parcel  in the post with a handwritten letter  none other than the duke of edinburgh  saying here's some sales you know enjoy  it i hope you have good fun with it  which i thought was a  lovely touch for someone who's so busy  my father was um  commanding officer on the trinity house  patricia at the time  in um hence he was obviously hosting  hosting the duke on board  it must have been nearly 20 years later  my father got invited to  um the garden party at buckingham palace  and he was able to take  two children so i went along whilst  uh the duke came down his line he  actually broke away from his equity  made a beeline for my dad and he came  over and  had quite an informal chat with us all  which was really really lovely  the duke was described to us today as a  legend  his legacy certainly hard to forget  catherine of totsi sky news
 

